We ask whether label-free feature footprints predict cross-operator transfer and whether they can define a smaller augmentation basis without sacrificing worst-case accuracy. Our footprint uses two observable coordinates: the increase in training-vocabulary misses and the disruption of character trigrams. We treat average edit count as a control, preventing a mechanically larger corruption from masquerading as a novel operator. We then cover the four operator footprints with the smallest epsilon-expanded convex hull and train on that basis under a fixed example budget. Here, ``minimal'' means fewer augmentation operator types, not fewer augmented examples or demonstrated training-time savings.

